1. Core Query Operations (mandatory)
🔹 Filtering (WHERE / $match)
Operation	Meaning
eq	equals
ne	not equals
gt	greater than
gte	greater or equal
lt	less than
lte	less or equal
in	in array
nin	not in array
exists	field exists
regex	pattern match
🔹 Logical Operators
Operation	Meaning
and	all conditions must match
or	any condition matches
not	negate condition

Example AST:

{
  "or": [
    { "field": "salary", "op": "gt", "value": 100000 },
    { "field": "role", "op": "eq", "value": "senior" }
  ]
}
2. Field Selection (SELECT / $project)
Operation	Meaning
include	include field
exclude	remove field
alias	rename field
computed	derived field

Example:

{
  "select": {
    "title": 1,
    "salary": 1,
    "companyName": { "field": "company.name" }
  }
}
3. Sorting (ORDER BY / $sort)
Operation	Meaning
asc	ascending
desc	descending

Example:

{
  "sort": {
    "salary": "desc",
    "createdAt": "asc"
  }
}
4. Pagination
Operation	SQL	Mongo
limit	LIMIT	$limit
offset	OFFSET	$skip

Example:

{
  "limit": 10,
  "offset": 20
}
5. Aggregation Functions
Operation	Meaning
count	COUNT(*)
sum	SUM
avg	AVG
min	MIN
max	MAX

Example:

{
  "aggregate": {
    "totalSalary": { "sum": "salary" },
    "avgSalary": { "avg": "salary" }
  }
}
6. Grouping ($group / GROUP BY)
Operation	Meaning
groupBy	group keys
having	filter grouped results

Example:

{
  "groupBy": ["department"],
  "having": [
    { "field": "salary", "op": "gt", "value": 50000 }
  ]
}
7. Joins / Relationships

This is where SQL vs Mongo diverge.

Operation	SQL	Mongo
join	JOIN	$lookup
leftJoin	LEFT JOIN	$lookup + preserve
innerJoin	INNER JOIN	filtered $lookup

Example:

{
  "join": {
    "type": "left",
    "from": "companies",
    "localField": "companyId",
    "foreignField": "_id",
    "as": "company"
  }
}
8. Array Operations (very Mongo-specific)
Operation	Meaning
unwind	flatten array
contains	array includes value
size	array length
all	contains all elements

Example:

{
  "unwind": "skills"
}
9. Nested Field Access
Operation	Meaning
dot	access nested field
jsonPath	deep JSON query

Example:

{
  "where": {
    "field": "company.location.city",
    "op": "eq",
    "value": "Delhi"
  }
}
10. Update Operations (optional extension)

If you extend beyond read queries:

Operation	Meaning
set	update field
unset	remove field
inc	increment
push	append to array
pull	remove from array
11. Advanced Logical Blocks (for full engine)
Operation	Meaning
caseWhen	SQL CASE WHEN
coalesce	first non-null
ifNull	fallback value
